tasks.prompt:
Render each template against this JSON data (values are never re-scanned for braces): {"user":{"name":"Ada","nick":"","tags":["x","y"],"age":36,"admin":false,"manager":null},"note":"use {braces} here","items":[{"name":"pen","qty":3},{"name":"ink","qty":0}]}
Scan a template left to right. At each position: if the text starts with {{ output one literal { and skip both characters; else if it starts with }} output one literal } and skip both; else if it starts with a single { , the placeholder body runs up to the next } (consumed) and is evaluated; any other character is copied. E.g. {{x}} gives {x}, and {{{x}}} gives { + value of x + }.
A body is PATH or PATH|FALLBACK, split at the first | only (FALLBACK may contain further | ). PATH is dot-separated: a segment indexes an object by key or a list by 0-based position. PATH is missing if any step fails or the final value is null; an empty string IS present. Present values print as is: strings raw, integers in decimal, booleans as true or false. If PATH is missing: with no FALLBACK print <?PATH>; if FALLBACK starts with @, look up the rest as a path and print it (nothing if also missing); otherwise print FALLBACK literally.
Templates:
1) Hi {user.name|guest}, {user.nick|@user.name}!
2) {{{user.name}}} {{user.name}} }}{{
3) {note} / {items.1.name}x{items.1.qty} / {items.0.name|none}
4) {user.manager|@user.name} {user.admin} {user.age} {user.tags.1} {user.tags.5|n/a} {user.zip}
5) [{user.nick|@user.manager}]|{user.zip|x|y}|{x|@y}
Return answer as a JSON array of the 5 rendered strings.

Apply an RFC 7386 JSON Merge Patch. Algorithm MergePatch(target, patch): (1) if patch is not an object, the result is patch itself (arrays and scalars replace wholesale). (2) Otherwise, if target is not an object (missing, scalar, array or null), treat target as {}. (3) For each key k in patch: if patch[k] is null, remove k from target (nothing happens if k is absent); otherwise set target[k] = MergePatch(target[k], patch[k]), where an absent target[k] counts as not an object. Null values already present in target, at keys the patch does not mention, are kept.
target = {"title":"Doc","tags":["x","y"],"author":{"name":"Joe","email":"j@x","phone":null},"meta":{"v":1,"flags":{"a":true,"b":false}},"stats":5,"notes":[1,{"k":2}]}
patch = {"title":null,"tags":{"first":"x"},"author":{"email":null,"phone":"555","nick":null},"meta":{"flags":{"b":null,"c":[]},"v":{"major":1,"minor":null}},"stats":[],"notes":{},"missing":null,"fresh":{"a":{"b":null},"c":3}}
Return answer as the resulting JSON object (key order is irrelevant; array order matters).

Compute merge(merge(base, o1), o2). merge(A, B): (a) B an object: if A is not an object use {}; for each key k of B: B[k] null -> delete k from the result; else result[k] = merge(A[k], B[k]) (an absent A[k] is neither object nor array). (b) A and B both arrays: if every element of both is an object with an "id" key (empty arrays qualify), merge by id: start with A's elements; for each B element in order, if an element with an equal id exists (same type and value) then remove it if the B element has "_delete": true, else replace it with merge(existing, B element); if none exists, append merge({}, B element) unless it has "_delete": true (then ignore it). Else if every element of both is a scalar (empty qualifies): A's elements followed by B's elements not already present (same type and value). Else the result is B. (c) Anything else: the result is B.
base = {"app":{"name":"core","ports":[80,443],"features":["a","b"],"limits":{"cpu":1,"mem":512},"hooks":[{"id":"h1","cmd":"x","env":{"A":"1","B":"2"}},{"id":"h2","cmd":"y"}]},"owner":"ops","labels":{"tier":"gold"},"retries":3}
o1 = {"app":{"ports":[443,8080,80,9000],"features":["c","a"],"limits":{"mem":null,"disk":10},"hooks":[{"id":"h1","env":{"B":null,"C":"3"}},{"id":"h3","cmd":"z"},{"id":"h2","_delete":true}]},"owner":{"team":"sre","pager":null},"labels":["x"],"retries":null,"extra":{"a":null,"b":[]}}
o2 = {"app":{"name":"core2","ports":[],"features":[1,"a"],"hooks":[{"id":"h3","cmd":null,"env":{"Z":"9"}},{"id":"h9","_delete":true},{"id":"h4","note":null},{"id":"h3","cmd":"w"}]},"owner":{"team":null,"pager":"p1"},"labels":[{"id":1,"v":2}],"extra":{"b":[{"id":7}]}}
Return answer as the final merged JSON object (key order is irrelevant; array order matters).

Compute the canonical diff turning A into B as a list of RFC 6902 ops (only add, remove, replace). diff(x, y, path): if both are objects, visit the union of their keys in ascending order of the RAW (unescaped) key by plain codepoint comparison (uppercase before lowercase, '-' (45) before '/' (47)); for each key: only in x -> remove; only in y -> add with y's value; in both -> diff(x[k], y[k], path + '/' + escaped key). If both are arrays, let m be the shorter length: diff elements 0..m-1 in order at path/i; then for i = m..len(y)-1 ascending emit add at path/i with y[i]; then for i = len(x)-1 down to m emit remove at path/i. Otherwise, if the two values are not equal, emit replace at path with y's whole value (this covers different types, null vs anything, and true vs 1; equality is deep, key order ignored). Equal values emit nothing. Paths are RFC 6901 pointers: escape '~' as ~0 first, then '/' as ~1. Objects are {"op","path","value"}; remove ops have no "value" key.
A = {"id":7,"Name":"x","a/b":{"k~":[1,2,3,4,5],"z":null},"a-b":true,"list":[{"n":1,"t":["p"]},{"n":2},5],"old":1,"flag":1}
B = {"id":7,"name":"x","a/b":{"k~":[1,9,3],"z":0,"w":{}},"a-b":true,"list":[{"n":1,"t":["p","q"]},[2],5,6,7],"flag":true,"new":[]}
Return answer as a JSON array of op objects in emission order, e.g. [{"op":"remove","path":"/x"},{"op":"add","path":"/y","value":1}].
